(2222990, 300598, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Let me preface my comments by saying this:  I don''t normally cut and paste comments, but I just finished a critique on a shot very similar to yours.  Since most of the points from that shot apply here, I''m going to use this approach.  I want you to know ahead of time so that if you look at my other comments you don''t feel cheated...<br/> <br/>I see that this is your very first entry into a challenge here at DPC. Please don''t let your low score discourage you from future participation!<br/> <br/>At first glance it appears to be just a snapshot. There is nothing wrong with a snapshot but they only really have value to the person who took them or those that know the subject well. To everyone else there is not really anything to hold their attention.<br/> <br/>This shot could be improved in many ways. The first thing is the angle that you shot from. This looks like you shot it from a standing or seated position and from slightly above the subject of the shot. In other words you shot it from YOUR point of view. When shooting kids and pets this doesn''t work so well. You need to get down to THEIR level and shoot them more directly! This may mean getting down on your hands and knees or even lying right on the floor. Try this sometime with your dog. Shoot him from a standing position then again from down at his level. You will see a significant improvement assuming you can keep the dog from licking the lens. :-P<br/> <br/>The next thing that would help to improve this would be to take out any element of the shot that doesn''t really ADD to the shot. In this case it would be the various different blankets and comforters on the bed.  The different colors don''t fit well together and even clash in places (dark blue against the white.)<br/> <br/>Your lighting here is not very creative. It looks like you used the flash on the camera. You can see this in the harsh shadow behind the dogs right ear (left in frame) and the way the coat glows where it faces the camera. It takes all the depth out of the shot and makes it appear very flat. One of the first things that I learned when I arrived here at DPC was to NEVER use the flash on the camera as is. Sometimes you need to use it but most times you can get away without it with some care. Use a tripod to keep the camera still with the slower shutter speed. Use other lights to light up the scene preferably from one side or the other. Use more than one light. One for the main lighting and another to fill in the darker side a touch (this could even be a simple reflecter like a piece of white poster board). If you think that you MUST use your flash you can difuse it a tad by taping a couple layers of tissue paper over the flash. This will help to soften it up.  You say that you tried for 2 days to catch a shot of the dog on your bed.  With a little planning, you could have your lighting set up ahead of time for just such a moment<br/> <br/>The angle that you shot from here is not very flattering to the dog.  One of the first things that I noticed is you shot right into his left ear (right in frame) and that''s not the most attractive part of a dog.  Choosing different angles to shoot from and taking several shots will give you more choices when picking the final shot.<br/> <br/>I know that this is a lot to think about when trying to take a picture but if you practice and take lots of pictures, soon it will be second nature! Once again, please don''t be discouraged by your score or your placing in the challenge. We all started somewhere and even the best photographers take bad shots once in a while! I''ll be looking forward to your future shots.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps<br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: Let me preface my comments by saying this:  I don''t normally cut and paste comments, but I just finished a critique on a shot very similar to yours.  Since most of the points from that shot apply here, I''m going to use this approach.  I want you to know ahead of time so that if you look at my other comments you don''t feel cheated... I see that this is your very first entry into a challenge here at DPC. Please don''t let your low score discourage you from future participation! At first glance it appears to be just a snapshot. There is nothing wrong with a snapshot but they only really have value to the person who took them or those that know the subject well. To everyone else there is not really anything to hold their attention. This shot could be improved in many ways. The first thing is the angle that you shot from. This looks like you shot it from a standing or seated position and from slightly above the subject of the shot. In other words you shot it from YOUR point of view. When shooting kids and pets this doesn''t work so well. You need to get down to THEIR level and shoot them more directly! This may mean getting down on your hands and knees or even lying right on the floor. Try this sometime with your dog. Shoot him from a standing position then again from down at his level. You will see a significant improvement assuming you can keep the dog from licking the lens. :-P The next thing that would help to improve this would be to take out any element of the shot that doesn''t really ADD to the shot. In this case it would be the various different blankets and comforters on the bed.  The different colors don''t fit well together and even clash in places (dark blue against the white.) Your lighting here is not very creative. It looks like you used the flash on the camera. You can see this in the harsh shadow behind the dogs right ear (left in frame) and the way the coat glows where it faces the camera. It takes all the depth out of the shot and makes it appear very flat. One of the first things that I learned when I arrived here at DPC was to NEVER use the flash on the camera as is. Sometimes you need to use it but most times you can get away without it with some care. Use a tripod to keep the camera still with the slower shutter speed. Use other lights to light up the scene preferably from one side or the other. Use more than one light. One for the main lighting and another to fill in the darker side a touch (this could even be a simple reflecter like a piece of white poster board). If you think that you MUST use your flash you can difuse it a tad by taping a couple layers of tissue paper over the flash. This will help to soften it up.  You say that you tried for 2 days to catch a shot of the dog on your bed.  With a little planning, you could have your lighting set up ahead of time for just such a moment The angle that you shot from here is not very flattering to the dog.  One of the first things that I noticed is you shot right into his left ear (right in frame) and that''s not the most attractive part of a dog.  Choosing different angles to shoot from and taking several shots will give you more choices when picking the final shot. I know that this is a lot to think about when trying to take a picture but if you practice and take lots of pictures, soon it will be second nature! Once again, please don''t be discouraged by your score or your placing in the challenge. We all started somewhere and even the best photographers take bad shots once in a while! I''ll be looking forward to your future shots. Hope this helpsYoursTC', '2006-03-12 22:43:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223118, 300942, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Obsidian, if you keep this rate of progress up, you''ll be on the front page in no time flat!<br/> <br/>It''s almost impossible to score well with a stuffed animal, yet you did it. Congratulations.  This challenge was made for your Bear Necessity.  The well-loved symbol of comfort - a properly mauled Bear - certainly struck a chord with our viewers.  And rightly so, I might add, as you presented the little appealing creature very well including the highlight in the eye.<br/> <br/>Nope, I have no ''suggestions'' as to how to do this image better.  You''ve done it all.<br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Obsidian, if you keep this rate of progress up, you''ll be on the front page in no time flat! It''s almost impossible to score well with a stuffed animal, yet you did it. Congratulations.  This challenge was made for your Bear Necessity.  The well-loved symbol of comfort - a properly mauled Bear - certainly struck a chord with our viewers.  And rightly so, I might add, as you presented the little appealing creature very well including the highlight in the eye. Nope, I have no ''suggestions'' as to how to do this image better.  You''ve done it all. Good luck in future challenges. Alice', '2006-03-12 23:27:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223917, 300125, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> And congratulation on high score on your very first submission.<br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> Pleazing. Zen like.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Well exposed, good colors, good contrast and sharp focus. Thats it.<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> This indeed is zen like - simple and still very srtong compositions. Colors are crisp and not harsh. The focus is sharp and DOF is perfect. For my taste the tone is little warmer though but who is complaining in this cold weather :) And yes- it certainly is good fit for comfort. The next thing I''m going to go after this critique is to hit the bed :)<br/> <br/>I dont really know what better can be done to this photo. Maybe a little different angle or crop might make it look different (if not any better)<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! And congratulation on high score on your very first submission. Aesthetic: Pleazing. Zen like. Technical: Well exposed, good colors, good contrast and sharp focus. Thats it. Conclusion: This indeed is zen like - simple and still very srtong compositions. Colors are crisp and not harsh. The focus is sharp and DOF is perfect. For my taste the tone is little warmer though but who is complaining in this cold weather :) And yes- it certainly is good fit for comfort. The next thing I''m going to go after this critique is to hit the bed :) I dont really know what better can be done to this photo. Maybe a little different angle or crop might make it look different (if not any better) I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-13 07:33:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223932, 301644, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> A nice pleasing portrait.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Color are crisp but focus needs bit of work. Also the image is little blownout at highlights.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> I think its closer to theme, but needed the title for direct association.<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Overall a good capture but could have been much better with better postprocessing. The focus seems to be little soft and shallow DOF has thrown the toy face out of focus. Also soft glow, in post-processing would have give soothing feeling to highlight "comfort". And yes - the model is cute :)<br/> <br/>I did not voted for this challenge but would have given anything between 5-6, which is in-line with final score. Goodluck for future submissions. You have got some amazing shots in your portfolio. "The Blood Hells -- Beppu, Japan" and "My Buddy" are too cool :)<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: A nice pleasing portrait. Technical: Color are crisp but focus needs bit of work. Also the image is little blownout at highlights. Challenge: I think its closer to theme, but needed the title for direct association. Conclusion: Overall a good capture but could have been much better with better postprocessing. The focus seems to be little soft and shallow DOF has thrown the toy face out of focus. Also soft glow, in post-processing would have give soothing feeling to highlight "comfort". And yes - the model is cute :) I did not voted for this challenge but would have given anything between 5-6, which is in-line with final score. Goodluck for future submissions. You have got some amazing shots in your portfolio. "The Blood Hells -- Beppu, Japan" and "My Buddy" are too cool :) I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-13 07:41:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223959, 300598, 45521, '<td valign="top">WOW... Thanks... It does make a lot to think of, but having comment like that is the only way I will improve... that and practice.<br/> <br/>Thanks again.<br/></td>', 'WOW... Thanks... It does make a lot to think of, but having comment like that is the only way I will improve... that and practice. Thanks again.', '2006-03-13 07:55:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223960, 300577, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> I think its a great scene which needs bit more attention for better results.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Seems to be well exposed but the focus seems to be on softer side.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> Closer to challenge and the title passes the idea well :)<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Overall this is average composition. It could have turned out much better if rule-of-thirds was followed. A little bit more of back-lighting would have given good silhoutte effect to bushes. Though I''m not too sure, but maybe 6x4 crop would have taken away extra negative space from top and bottom of frame.<br/> <br/>I did not voted for this challenge but would have given around 5 to this one which is in-line with overall score. Considering this is just your second submission, you surely have potential :) Good-luck for future submissions!<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: I think its a great scene which needs bit more attention for better results. Technical: Seems to be well exposed but the focus seems to be on softer side. Challenge: Closer to challenge and the title passes the idea well :) Conclusion: Overall this is average composition. It could have turned out much better if rule-of-thirds was followed. A little bit more of back-lighting would have given good silhoutte effect to bushes. Though I''m not too sure, but maybe 6x4 crop would have taken away extra negative space from top and bottom of frame. I did not voted for this challenge but would have given around 5 to this one which is in-line with overall score. Considering this is just your second submission, you surely have potential :) Good-luck for future submissions! I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-13 07:55:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2224231, 299611, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Welcome to DPC!  <br/> <br/>You have chosen a different interpretation of the Comfort Challenge, and it looks as if it was received with mixed response by our voters.<br/> <br/>Technically, a still life of this sort usually is tack sharp, especially in the foregound, but your aperture of 4.8 offered up a shallow depth of field with "Dizziness" being the sharpest point.  When viewers must puzzle out what is directly in front of them, they sometimes just don''t take the time to do so.  And of course, "Dizziness" doesn''t exactly spell comfort to some voters.<br/> <br/>Just one last thing - when you look at the top placement images on DPC, you may notice that many of them are extremely simple.  you managed to put quite a bit of detail in this image, and my suggestion would be to try to recreate this image removing information until the minimum is reached to tell your story.<br/> <br/>Okay, I''m running off at the mouth this morning, and probably should take my own medicine and simple down this critique. :)<br/> <br/>But I''m going to send it along anyhow.<br/> <br/>I wish you well in future Challenges and look forward to seeing more of your work.  You have a unique way of seeing and that goes a long way at DPC.<br/> <br/>Alice<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Welcome to DPC!   You have chosen a different interpretation of the Comfort Challenge, and it looks as if it was received with mixed response by our voters. Technically, a still life of this sort usually is tack sharp, especially in the foregound, but your aperture of 4.8 offered up a shallow depth of field with "Dizziness" being the sharpest point.  When viewers must puzzle out what is directly in front of them, they sometimes just don''t take the time to do so.  And of course, "Dizziness" doesn''t exactly spell comfort to some voters. Just one last thing - when you look at the top placement images on DPC, you may notice that many of them are extremely simple.  you managed to put quite a bit of detail in this image, and my suggestion would be to try to recreate this image removing information until the minimum is reached to tell your story. Okay, I''m running off at the mouth this morning, and probably should take my own medicine and simple down this critique. :) But I''m going to send it along anyhow. I wish you well in future Challenges and look forward to seeing more of your work.  You have a unique way of seeing and that goes a long way at DPC. Alice ', '2006-03-13 10:12:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2224496, 300626, 32321, '<td valign="top">Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>First Impression:<br/>My eyes went to the top left green tiles, black ears of kitten and then down to the cat. Green busy and distracting<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>Location and expression of the cats is good.<br/>Step tread and background overpower the cats.<br/> <br/>Subject:<br/>Good capture and well titled.<br/> <br/>Technical:<br/>DOF shallow, focus on kitten is soft.<br/>Not sure why you used 800 ISO, main subjects, kitten and cat lack detail.<br/>Picture not square. Slight drop from horizontal on left side.<br/> <br/>Summary:<br/>The capture is good, unfortunately the location worked against you.<br/></td>', 'Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club. First Impression:My eyes went to the top left green tiles, black ears of kitten and then down to the cat. Green busy and distracting Composition:Location and expression of the cats is good.Step tread and background overpower the cats. Subject:Good capture and well titled. Technical:DOF shallow, focus on kitten is soft.Not sure why you used 800 ISO, main subjects, kitten and cat lack detail.Picture not square. Slight drop from horizontal on left side. Summary:The capture is good, unfortunately the location worked against you.', '2006-03-13 12:28:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(2224952, 300726, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hello!  I''ve just wandered through your other Challenge entries, and lunix, I Love the way you see things and photograph them. You have a real artist''s eye.  Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>This subject, photographed in your own unqiue style, is beautifully captured.  <br/> <br/>Unfortunately, it does not meet the preconceived concept of "Comfort" in the minds of our voters.  Thus it did not score well.  <br/> <br/>Not Your Fault! <br/> <br/>I can easily see that this piece of furniture, decrepit as it may be now, once held innumerable people in comfort.  And even now, next to the dust bin, it does have that stately bit of charm that attracted you and now, after looking at it, me.<br/> <br/>So, thank you for finding this and photographing it.  I shall look forward to seeing more of your work.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Hello!  I''ve just wandered through your other Challenge entries, and lunix, I Love the way you see things and photograph them. You have a real artist''s eye.  Keep up the good work. This subject, photographed in your own unqiue style, is beautifully captured.   Unfortunately, it does not meet the preconceived concept of "Comfort" in the minds of our voters.  Thus it did not score well.   Not Your Fault!  I can easily see that this piece of furniture, decrepit as it may be now, once held innumerable people in comfort.  And even now, next to the dust bin, it does have that stately bit of charm that attracted you and now, after looking at it, me. So, thank you for finding this and photographing it.  I shall look forward to seeing more of your work. Alice', '2006-03-13 15:40:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2224980, 299751, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>Hi joycobb :)<br/> <br/>This photo doesn''t seem to require much of a critique.  It scored rather well.  This photo meets the challenge very well overall.  The only critique that I can possibly offer is that the lighting is a bit harsh and flat with the flash.  Some directional light or natural light would have possibly produced a much more moody image.  Some warm toning to the black and white would have also added an extra sense of ''comfort'' to the image :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club... Hi joycobb :) This photo doesn''t seem to require much of a critique.  It scored rather well.  This photo meets the challenge very well overall.  The only critique that I can possibly offer is that the lighting is a bit harsh and flat with the flash.  Some directional light or natural light would have possibly produced a much more moody image.  Some warm toning to the black and white would have also added an extra sense of ''comfort'' to the image :) John Setzler', '2006-03-13 15:48:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2225051, 299751, 15711, '<td valign="top">SWEET!!!  Nice contrast and very clear.</td>', 'SWEET!!!  Nice contrast and very clear.', '2006-03-13 16:17:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2225737, 301566, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Comfy just hanging''" is a nice addition to the Comfort challenge.  The image itself <br/>is nice and comfortable. Pretty decent placement in a hard category to capture.<br/> <br/>I think the hardest part about this image is the focus.   I think the focus shows more so because of the hair on the slippers is enhanced by the light.  The colors are nice and balance each other out.  The contrast between the red background and the purple slippers draws more attention into our focus.  The lighting is very nice and accents the piece wonderfully.  Great idea and nice implementation.  I really think if the shoes were in focus and we let the rest fade to a soft background the score would have been higher.  Nice idea.<br/> <br/></td>', '*Critique Club* "Comfy just hanging''" is a nice addition to the Comfort challenge.  The image itself is nice and comfortable. Pretty decent placement in a hard category to capture. I think the hardest part about this image is the focus.   I think the focus shows more so because of the hair on the slippers is enhanced by the light.  The colors are nice and balance each other out.  The contrast between the red background and the purple slippers draws more attention into our focus.  The lighting is very nice and accents the piece wonderfully.  Great idea and nice implementation.  I really think if the shoes were in focus and we let the rest fade to a soft background the score would have been higher.  Nice idea. ', '2006-03-13 21:13:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2225761, 300936, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>" He''s up there" is a nice idea into the Comfort challenge.  I have to give you credit, the first challenge is always the hardest to enter.<br/> <br/>One of the first things I have learned about the challenges is no matter how good or bad your shot, if you don''t utilize the entire space limit the voters will score you down.<br/> <br/>You have a good idea and subject.  That is defiantly a fundamental piece to a good entry.  If you are going to use layers like the bushes in the front and clouds in background I would make sure one is very in focus.  In this piece I would leave the bushes in focus. Also in the image it does look a little grainy, possibly noise from the lighting.  Shots with sunlight are hard.<br/> <br/>Overall its a great idea, I really hope despite some of the comments you continue to enter.  The more you enter the better you get.</td>', '*Critique Club* " He''s up there" is a nice idea into the Comfort challenge.  I have to give you credit, the first challenge is always the hardest to enter. One of the first things I have learned about the challenges is no matter how good or bad your shot, if you don''t utilize the entire space limit the voters will score you down. You have a good idea and subject.  That is defiantly a fundamental piece to a good entry.  If you are going to use layers like the bushes in the front and clouds in background I would make sure one is very in focus.  In this piece I would leave the bushes in focus. Also in the image it does look a little grainy, possibly noise from the lighting.  Shots with sunlight are hard. Overall its a great idea, I really hope despite some of the comments you continue to enter.  The more you enter the better you get.', '2006-03-13 21:26:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2226101, 300677, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Well exposed photo which has lots of potantial if played in post-processing.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> Cannot be tightly coupled with theme, but the title gives the hint.<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Overall a good photo technically, but needed more work in postprocessing. If you notice carefully, your "whites" are not really white but are a little grey. You need to do white point adjustment for this one and it will also improve on the contrast. Since the subject is not very unique, its good to have some different angle or crop to make such shots more interesting. <br/> <br/>You have got some real nice wildlife pictures in your profile. Goodluck for future submissions.<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Technical: Well exposed photo which has lots of potantial if played in post-processing. Challenge: Cannot be tightly coupled with theme, but the title gives the hint. Conclusion: Overall a good photo technically, but needed more work in postprocessing. If you notice carefully, your "whites" are not really white but are a little grey. You need to do white point adjustment for this one and it will also improve on the contrast. Since the subject is not very unique, its good to have some different angle or crop to make such shots more interesting.  You have got some real nice wildlife pictures in your profile. Goodluck for future submissions. I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej ', '2006-03-14 00:32:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2226111, 300044, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/>Someone seems to be in love with his cat :). And why not - she is such a beauty.<br/> <br/>Coming to photo- I feel it is an average composition. The subjects beauty is not really getting highlighted and somehow it does not click the word "comfort" at first look.<br/> <br/>What would have worked -<br/>-Better Crop (with more of subject and less of distracting elements like rug or clothes)<br/>-Better Contrast (Post-processing can do wonders to such well exposed photo)<br/> <br/>When the subject is not-so-unique, always try to make the photo unique by trying different angle, crop and even post-processing. Some people do wonders with simple dodge-burn on ''common'' subjects. <br/> <br/>Goodluck for future submissions!<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Someone seems to be in love with his cat :). And why not - she is such a beauty. Coming to photo- I feel it is an average composition. The subjects beauty is not really getting highlighted and somehow it does not click the word "comfort" at first look. What would have worked --Better Crop (with more of subject and less of distracting elements like rug or clothes)-Better Contrast (Post-processing can do wonders to such well exposed photo) When the subject is not-so-unique, always try to make the photo unique by trying different angle, crop and even post-processing. Some people do wonders with simple dodge-burn on ''common'' subjects.  Goodluck for future submissions! I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-14 00:38:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2226120, 299439, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> If I had to ignore the theme, and technical stuff, this could have come out to better than what it scored. Its pleasing but lacks on few fronts.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> The colors are bright (which is good) but the focus is soft (or is it the motion blur??). If its blur, because of moving subject, that higher ISO or shutter sheep would have been helpful.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> Happiness is always comforting :) but somehow the image in itself is not tightly coupled with feeling of "comfort". Sometimes its good to give complimentary title to convery the "theme".<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Overall an average composition. You have got eye for "aesthatics" but need to work on equipment and angles. Sharp focus and little tighter crop (to make the face and smile more visible) would have helped. I did not voted for this challenge but would have given 5 which is inline with final score.<br/> <br/>I checked on your profile and you have some great pictures in there. I really liked the "tribute" submission. You have got eye for beauty and you are lucky to have got models to compliment that :) Goodluck for future submissions.<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: If I had to ignore the theme, and technical stuff, this could have come out to better than what it scored. Its pleasing but lacks on few fronts. Technical: The colors are bright (which is good) but the focus is soft (or is it the motion blur??). If its blur, because of moving subject, that higher ISO or shutter sheep would have been helpful. Challenge: Happiness is always comforting :) but somehow the image in itself is not tightly coupled with feeling of "comfort". Sometimes its good to give complimentary title to convery the "theme". Conclusion: Overall an average composition. You have got eye for "aesthatics" but need to work on equipment and angles. Sharp focus and little tighter crop (to make the face and smile more visible) would have helped. I did not voted for this challenge but would have given 5 which is inline with final score. I checked on your profile and you have some great pictures in there. I really liked the "tribute" submission. You have got eye for beauty and you are lucky to have got models to compliment that :) Goodluck for future submissions. I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej ', '2006-03-14 00:48:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2226296, 300323, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> -Skip-<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> -Skip-<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> -Skip-<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Overall an average composition which does not go very well with the theme of challenge. Sometimes such pictures do well, but than they need to be technically or aesthatically "exceptional". Maybe a different crop, with less negative space could have helped. Considering this is your first submission, you have scored fair on this one.<br/> <br/>Looking forward to more submissions from you and wishing you good-luck for future!<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: -Skip- Technical: -Skip- Challenge: -Skip- Conclusion: Overall an average composition which does not go very well with the theme of challenge. Sometimes such pictures do well, but than they need to be technically or aesthatically "exceptional". Maybe a different crop, with less negative space could have helped. Considering this is your first submission, you have scored fair on this one. Looking forward to more submissions from you and wishing you good-luck for future! I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-14 01:50:03.000000', NULL, 0),
